small ornamental skewers or needles (''aiguilles'').

'''aiguisé''' (ā-gwē-zā′), ''a.'' [F., pp. of ''aiguiser'', sharpen, = Pr. ''agusar'' = It. ''aguzzare'', < ML. ''acutiare'', sharpen, < L. ''acutus'', sharp: see ''acute''.] In ''her.'', sharpened or pointed: applied to anything sharpened, but in such manner as to terminate in an obtuse angle. Synonymous with ''appointée''. Also written ''éguisé''.

'''aigulet''' (ā′gū-let), ''n.'' Same as ''aglet'', 1: as, “golden ''aygulets'',” ''Spenser'', F. Q., II. iii. 26.

'''aikinite''' (ā′kin-īt), ''n.'' [Named after Dr. A. ''Aikin''.] A native sulphid of bismuth, lead, and copper, of a metallic luster and blackish lead-gray color. {{smaller|It commonly occurs in embedded acicular crystals, and is hence called ''needle-ore'' and ''acicular bismuth''.}}

'''ail<sup>1</sup>†''', ''a.'' [< ME. ''eyle'', ''eil'', < AS. ''egle'', painful, troublesome, = Goth. ''aglus'', hard. Cf. Goth. ''aglo'', distress, tribulation, akin to ''agis'', fright, = E. ''awe''<sup>1</sup>, q. v.] Painful; troublesome.
{{smaller block|''Eyle'' and hard and muche.
{{right|''Castle of Love'', l. 223.}}}}

'''ail<sup>1</sup>''' (āl), ''v.'' [< ME. ''ailen'', ''aylen'', earlier ''eilen'', ''eylen'', ''eȝlen'', < AS. ''eglian'', ''eglan'', trouble, pain, = Goth. *''agljan'', only in comp. ''usagljan'', trouble exceedingly, distress; from the adj.: see ''ail''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.'' and ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To affect with pain or uneasiness, either of body or of mind; trouble: used in relation to some uneasiness or affection whose cause is unknown: as, what ''ails'' the man?
{{smaller block|What ''aileth'' thee, Hagar? {{right|Gen. xxi. 17.}}
What do you ''ail'', my love? why do you weep?
{{right|''Webster'', The White Devil, iv. 2.}}
Never rave nor rail,}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|Nor ask questions what I ''ail.''
{{right|''Peele,'' Edward I. (Dyce ed., 1861), p. 395.}}}}
{{smaller block|[Rarely used with a specific disease as subject, unless colloquially in iterative answer to a question: as, “What ''ails'' you? A pleurisy ''ails'' me.”]}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To feel pain; be ill (usually in a slight degree); be unwell: now used chiefly in the present participle: as, he is ''ailing'' to-day.
{{smaller block|And much he ''ails,'' and yet he is not sick.
{{right|''Daniel,'' Civil Wars, iii.}}}}
{{smaller block|One day the child began to ''ail.''
{{right|''R. H. Stoddard,'' Pearl of the Philippines.}}}}

'''ail<sup>1</sup>''' (āl), ''n.'' [From the verb. Cf. early ME. ''eile, eil,'' harm (very rare); from the adj.] Indisposition or morbid affection; ailment. ''Pope.''

'''ail<sup>2</sup>''' (āl), ''n.'' [E. dial., in pl. ''ails;'' variously corrupted ''oils, hoils, hauels;'' < ME. ''eyle, eile, eiȝle,'' < AS. ''egl,'' the beard of grain, corn, found only twice, as tr. of L. ''festuca,'' “the ''mote'' that is in thy brother’s eye” (Luke vi. 41, 42), = OHG. ''ahil,'' G. ''achel,'' beard of grain; from the same root, with diff. suffix (''-l''), as ''awn''<sup>1</sup> and ''ear''<sup>2</sup>, q. v.] The beard of wheat, barley, etc., especially of barley: chiefly in the plural. ''Halliwell; Wright.'' [Prov. Eng. (Essex).]
{{smaller block|For to winden [''var.'' windwe, winnow] hweate, and scheaden [shed, ''i. e.,'' separate] the ''eilen'' and tet chef [the chaff] urom the clene cornes.
{{right|''Ancren Riwle,'' p. 270. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

'''ailantic, ailanthic''' (ā-lan′tik, -thik), ''a.'' [< ''Ailantus, Ailanthus,'' + ''-ic.''] Of or pertaining to ''Ailantus.''—{{smaller|'''Ailantic acid,''' an acid obtained from the bark of ''Ailantus excelsa.''}}

'''ailantine''' (ā-lan′tin), ''a.'' [< ''ailantus'' + ''-ine''<sup>1</sup>.] Relating or pertaining to the ailantus, or to the silkworms which feed upon its leaves.

