{{smaller block|<poem>It ill beseems this presence to ''cry aim''
To these ill-tuned repetitions.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1.}}}}

¶
'''aim''' (ām), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ayme'', ''ame'', < OF. ''esme''; from the verb.] '''1†.''' Conjecture; guess.
{{smaller block|He that seeth no mark, must shoot by ''aim''.
{{right|''Bp. Jewell'', Reply to Hardinge, p. 31.}}
It is impossible by ''aim'' to tell it.
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}
What you would work me to, I have some ''aim''.
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' Course; direction: in particular, the direction in which a missile is pointed; the line of shot.
{{smaller block|<poem>And when the cross-blue lightning seem’d to open
The breast of heaven, I did present myself
Even in the ''aim'' and very flash of it.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' The act of aiming or directing anything (as a weapon, a blow, a discourse, or a remark) at or toward a particular point or object with the intention of striking or affecting it; the pointing or directing of a missile.
{{smaller block|<poem>Each at the head
Levell’d his deadly ''aim''.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 712.}}}}
'''4.''' The point intended to be hit, or object intended to be affected; the mark or target.
{{smaller block|To be the ''aim'' of every dangerous shot.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iv. 4.}}}}
'''5.''' A purpose; intention; design; scheme: as, men are often disappointed of their ''aim''.
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''aim'', if reached or not, makes great the life.
Try to be Shakspeare, leave the rest to fate.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Bishop Blougram’s Apology.}}
The ''aim'' of scientific thought, then, is to apply past experiences to new circumstances.}}
